Resources - Ranked 3rd

- Smart Motorways – AA and RAC have large customer bases and resources to conduct large surveys and gather evidence 
- Stop HS2 – Organisation of MP support 
- XR – Highly organised with community groups and positions all around the country 
- Lobbying groups and businesses with strong financial resources can provide easy access to decision makers 
- Powerful businesses can employ high-profile figures to represent their interests. Since 2022, Sir Nick Clegg has been president of Facebook’s global affairs 
- If the governments views are incompataible wealth is unlikely to influence. Taxpayers alliance was unable to stop Johnson government 
- Celebrity endorsements can matter more than wealth e.g. Marcus Rashford

Ideological factors - ranked 1st

- Stop the war coalition - even though they had 1 million marching the governments ideology was more important due to their 179-seat majority. 
- XR protests - nothing has happened over climate change so they clearly lack success, and this can be put down to the bipartisan disliking of their actions 
- Smart Motorways – Used insider status to get audience with Transport Select Commitee to directly influence policy – government can agree with car safety being important 
- Stop HS2 – had cross party support from MPs with effected constituencies but overall parliament supported HS2, so they were ineffective. MPs voted 399 to 42 in favour of building HS2, and 386 to 26 in the lords

Popularity - Ranked 4th

- Rashford was a committed and engaging celebrity (Manchester United footballer with 3+ million followers on twitter) whose personal story resonated with the issue that was being discussed. His position spread the issue to many people and his social media presence allowed him to coordinate the efforts. He created an E-Petition that gained 1.1million signatures. 120£ million summer fund and 170£ million winter fund created by government. 
- Stop the war coalition - over 1 million marched through London. Blair had a 179-seat majority so ignored it. 
- XR - the large membership numbers of XR shows some level of public sympathy towards them but this has lacked to little policy success 
- Stop HS2 - 155,253 signatures on an E- Petition means there is clearly public support, but this failed to make any changes in the commons

Expertise - Ranked 2nd

- Free school meals - the charity Fareshare’s involvement allowed the campaign to be coordinated well and be effective. 
- Smart motorways – In 2021 the AA and the RAC, together with West Midlands police and Highways England, provided specialist evidence to the transport select committee. The government delayed building smart motorways for 5 years. 
- Stop HS2 – MPs joining meant they had expertise in legislatures and individual constituencies – still didn’t help enough
